#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698493 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf a=\\langle x,1,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$. Given that $|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|=\\sqrt{5}$, find all real values of $x$.\n\nUse the determinant setup\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\x&1&0\\\\0&0&1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the magnitude of $\\langle u,v,w\\rangle$ is $\\sqrt{u^2+v^2+w^2}$." :question/worked-solution "Expanding the determinant along its first row gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=(1\\cdot1-0\\cdot0)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad-(x\\cdot1-0\\cdot0)\\mathbf j\\\\\n&\\quad+(x\\cdot0-1\\cdot0)\\mathbf k\\\\\n&=\\langle1,-x,0\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the given magnitude to form an equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{1^2+(-x)^2+0^2}&=\\sqrt5\\\\\n1+x^2&=5\\\\\nx^2&=4\\\\\nx&=\\pm2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth signs are needed: when $x=2$ or $x=-2$, the magnitude is $\\sqrt{1+4}=\\sqrt5$. Therefore all real solutions are $x=-2$ and $x=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698499 :question/problem "Find $g_y(x,y,z)$ for $g(x,y,z)=(x+z)\\sin y+(y+z)\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, holding $x$ and $z$ fixed. Thus $x+z$ and $\\cos x$ are constants, while $y+z$ has derivative $1$.\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_y(x,y,z)&=(x+z)\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\sin y)+\\cos x\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(y+z)\\\\\n&=(x+z)\\cos y+\\cos x(1+0)\\\\\n&=(x+z)\\cos y+\\cos x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698506 :question/problem "For the function $h(x,y,z)=z\\sqrt{4x^2+y^2},$ evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(-2,3,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, holding $y$ and $z$ fixed. The factor $z$ stays constant, and the square root requires the chain rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}&=z\\cdot\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4x^2+y^2}}\\cdot\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(4x^2+y^2)\\\\\n&=\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{4x^2+y^2}}(8x+0)\\\\\n&=\\frac{4xz}{\\sqrt{4x^2+y^2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=-2$, $y=3$, and $z=-1$. The radicand is $25>0$, so this derivative is defined at the point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}\\right|_{(-2,3,-1)}&=\\frac{4(-2)(-1)}{\\sqrt{4(-2)^2+3^2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{25}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{8}{5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698508 :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ for $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+z^3.$ Treat $x$ and $z$ as constants." :question/worked-solution "Hold $x$ and $z$ fixed. The factor $x$ is a constant multiplier, and $z^3$ is independent of $y$.\n\nUsing the sum, constant multiple, and power rules,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=x\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(y^2)+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(z^3)\\\\\n&=x(2y)+0\\\\\n&=2xy.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698612 :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ for $f(x,y)=x^2y\\sin x+y^3.$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant. The term $y^3$ therefore has derivative zero, and $y$ is a constant multiplier in the first term:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=y\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2\\sin x)+0.\n$$\n\nApply the product rule to $x^2\\sin x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=y\\left(\\frac{\\partial x^2}{\\partial x}\\sin x+x^2\\frac{\\partial\\sin x}{\\partial x}\\right)\\\\\n&=y(2x\\sin x+x^2\\cos x).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth product-rule terms are needed; differentiating both factors and multiplying their derivatives would omit them."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698613 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=(x+y^2)(e^{xy}+\\ln y)$, evaluate $f_y(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Treat $x$ as constant. Both grouped factors depend on $y$, so neither entire factor is a constant multiplier. Let\n\n$$\nu=x+y^2,\\qquad v=e^{xy}+\\ln y.\n$$\n\nTheir partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_y&=2y,\\\\\nv_y&=e^{xy}\\frac{\\partial(xy)}{\\partial y}+\\frac{1}{y}\\\\\n&=xe^{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product rule gives\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y)=2y(e^{xy}+\\ln y)+(x+y^2)\\left(xe^{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}\\right).\n$$\n\nAt $(2,1)$, we have $\\ln1=0$ and $e^{xy}=e^2$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(2,1)&=2(e^2+0)+(2+1)(2e^2+1)\\\\\n&=2e^2+6e^2+3\\\\\n&=8e^2+3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough $\\ln1=0$, its derivative contributes $1/y=1$ at this point. That contribution must be included before evaluating."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698621 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=ye^{xy}-x^2y^2$, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, keeping $x$ constant. Use the product rule on $ye^{xy}$ and the constant multiple rule on $x^2y^2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=1\\cdot e^{xy}+y\\frac{\\partial e^{xy}}{\\partial y}-2x^2y.\n$$\n\nThe exponential also requires the chain rule. Since $\\dfrac{\\partial(xy)}{\\partial y}=x$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=e^{xy}+y e^{xy}x-2x^2y\\\\\n&=(1+xy)e^{xy}-2x^2y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(2,1)&=(1+2\\cdot1)e^{2\\cdot1}-2\\cdot2^2\\cdot1\\\\\n&=3e^2-8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep the $y$ dependence until after differentiating. Substituting both coordinates first would leave only a number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698669 :question/problem "Let $a=-4i-j-3k$ and $b=3i+2k$. Use a determinant to find $a\\times b$." :question/worked-solution "Read the coefficients in $i,j,k$ order: $a=\\langle -4,-1,-3\\rangle$ and $b=\\langle 3,0,2\\rangle$. Place $a$ above $b$ and expand along the top row with signs $+,-,+$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na\\times b&=\\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\\\-4&-1&-3\\\\3&0&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}-1&-3\\\\0&2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-4&-3\\\\3&2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-4&-1\\\\3&0\\end{vmatrix}k\\\\&=((-1)(2)-(-3)(0))i\\\\&\\quad-((-4)(2)-(-3)(3))j\\\\&\\quad+((-4)(0)-(-1)(3))k\\\\&=((-2)-(0))i\\\\&\\quad-((-8)-(-9))j\\\\&\\quad+((0)-(-3))k\\\\&=-2i-j+3k\\\\&=\\langle -2,-1,3\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698671 :question/problem "Let $a=2i-2j-2k$ and $b=i-2k$. Use a determinant to find $b\\times a$." :question/worked-solution "Read the coefficients in $i,j,k$ order: $a=\\langle 2,-2,-2\\rangle$ and $b=\\langle 1,0,-2\\rangle$. Place $b$ above $a$ and expand along the top row with signs $+,-,+$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb\\times a&=\\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\\\1&0&-2\\\\2&-2&-2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}0&-2\\\\-2&-2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1&-2\\\\2&-2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1&0\\\\2&-2\\end{vmatrix}k\\\\&=((0)(-2)-(-2)(-2))i\\\\&\\quad-((1)(-2)-(-2)(2))j\\\\&\\quad+((1)(-2)-(0)(2))k\\\\&=((0)-(4))i\\\\&\\quad-((-2)-(-4))j\\\\&\\quad+((-2)-(0))k\\\\&=-4i-2j-2k\\\\&=\\langle -4,-2,-2\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698674 :question/problem "Let $a=\\langle 0,1,-4\\rangle$ and $b=\\langle -1,3,-1\\rangle$. Use a determinant to find $a\\times b$." :question/worked-solution "Read the coefficients in $i,j,k$ order: $a=\\langle 0,1,-4\\rangle$ and $b=\\langle -1,3,-1\\rangle$. Place $a$ above $b$ and expand along the top row with signs $+,-,+$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na\\times b&=\\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\\\0&1&-4\\\\-1&3&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-4\\\\3&-1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}0&-4\\\\-1&-1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&3\\end{vmatrix}k\\\\&=((1)(-1)-(-4)(3))i\\\\&\\quad-((0)(-1)-(-4)(-1))j\\\\&\\quad+((0)(3)-(1)(-1))k\\\\&=((-1)-(-12))i\\\\&\\quad-((0)-(4))j\\\\&\\quad+((0)-(-1))k\\\\&=11i+4j+k\\\\&=\\langle 11,4,1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698679 :question/problem "Let $a=3i+2j-3k$ and $b=3k$. Use a determinant to find $b\\times a$." :question/worked-solution "Read the coefficients in $i,j,k$ order: $a=\\langle 3,2,-3\\rangle$ and $b=\\langle 0,0,3\\rangle$. Place $b$ above $a$ and expand along the top row with signs $+,-,+$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb\\times a&=\\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\\\0&0&3\\\\3&2&-3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}0&3\\\\2&-3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}0&3\\\\3&-3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}0&0\\\\3&2\\end{vmatrix}k\\\\&=((0)(-3)-(3)(2))i\\\\&\\quad-((0)(-3)-(3)(3))j\\\\&\\quad+((0)(2)-(0)(3))k\\\\&=((0)-(6))i\\\\&\\quad-((0)-(9))j\\\\&\\quad+((0)-(0))k\\\\&=-6i+9j\\\\&=\\langle -6,9,0\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698692 :question/problem "Let $a=3i+4j+4k$ and $b=4j$. Use a determinant to find $b\\times a$." :question/worked-solution "Read the coefficients in $i,j,k$ order: $a=\\langle 3,4,4\\rangle$ and $b=\\langle 0,4,0\\rangle$. Place $b$ above $a$ and expand along the top row with signs $+,-,+$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb\\times a&=\\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\\\0&4&0\\\\3&4&4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}4&0\\\\4&4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}0&0\\\\3&4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}0&4\\\\3&4\\end{vmatrix}k\\\\&=((4)(4)-(0)(4))i\\\\&\\quad-((0)(4)-(0)(3))j\\\\&\\quad+((0)(4)-(4)(3))k\\\\&=((16)-(0))i\\\\&\\quad-((0)-(0))j\\\\&\\quad+((0)-(12))k\\\\&=16i-12k\\\\&=\\langle 16,0,-12\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698731 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\ln(1+e^{xy})+x^2y,\n$$\n\nevaluate both partial derivatives at $(1,2)$.\n\n$f_x(1,2)=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$f_y(1,2)=$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For $f_x$, treat $y$ as a constant. The logarithmic term requires the chain rule twice: first through the logarithm, then through the exponential. The term $x^2y$ uses the constant multiple rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^{xy}}\\cdot e^{xy}\\cdot\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(xy)+2xy\\\\\n&=\\frac{ye^{xy}}{1+e^{xy}}+2xy.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $f_y$, instead treat $x$ as a constant. In particular, the inner derivative of $xy$ is now $x$, and the derivative of $x^2y$ is $x^2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^{xy}}\\cdot e^{xy}\\cdot\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(xy)+x^2\\\\\n&=\\frac{xe^{xy}}{1+e^{xy}}+x^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate each derivative at $x=1$, $y=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(1,2)&=\\frac{2e^2}{1+e^2}+2\\cdot1\\cdot2=\\frac{2e^2}{1+e^2}+4,\\\\\nf_y(1,2)&=\\frac{e^2}{1+e^2}+1^2=\\frac{e^2}{1+e^2}+1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two values differ because a different variable is held constant in each derivative. Also, $1+e^{xy}>0$ for all real $x,y$, so the logarithm and both derivatives are defined at the given point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698737 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=e^{xy}+x^2y$, evaluate $f_y(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant. Use the sum rule, then the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(e^{xy})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2y)\\\\\n&=e^{xy}\\cdot\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(xy)+x^2\\cdot\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial y}\\\\\n&=xe^{xy}+x^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=2$ and $y=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(2,0)&=2e^{2\\cdot0}+2^2\\\\\n&=2\\cdot1+4\\\\\n&=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative is taken before evaluating; substituting both coordinates into $f$ first would leave only a number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698745 :question/problem "Find $f_y(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=\\ln(x+y^2).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant. Let $u=x+y^2$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\ln u=\\frac{1}{u},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0+2y.\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{x+y^2}\\cdot\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x+y^2)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{x+y^2}\\cdot 2y\\\\\n&=\\frac{2y}{x+y^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe formula applies where $x+y^2>0$, the domain of the logarithm."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698770 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^2-y^2}{e^{y-x}}$, evaluate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(1,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant. In particular, the chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}e^{y-x}=-e^{y-x}.\n$$\n\nApply the quotient rule before evaluating at the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\n&=\\frac{2x e^{y-x}-(x^2-y^2)(-e^{y-x})}{(e^{y-x})^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{2x+x^2-y^2}{e^{y-x}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\right|_{(1,0)}\n=\\frac{2+1-0}{e^{-1}}=3e.\n$$\n\nThe denominator is always positive, so the function and this derivative are defined at the given point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698771 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{e^{xy^2}}{x+y^2},\n$$\n\nfind $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$, where $x+y^2\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $x$ constant. The numerator contains a composite function, so use the chain rule to differentiate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}e^{xy^2}\n&=e^{xy^2}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(xy^2)=2xy e^{xy^2}, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x+y^2)&=2y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth numerator and denominator depend on $y$. Apply the quotient rule, preserving the subtraction and the squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\n&=\\frac{(2xy e^{xy^2})(x+y^2)-e^{xy^2}(2y)}{(x+y^2)^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{2y e^{xy^2}\\bigl(x(x+y^2)-1\\bigr)}{(x+y^2)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $2y e^{xy^2}$ is common to the numerator terms; it cannot be canceled with the sum $x+y^2$ in the denominator. The derivative is defined wherever $x+y^2\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698774 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2x+3y}{x-2y}$. The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{(x-2y)^2}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant. The numerator and denominator derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(2x+3y)=3,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x-2y)=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\n&=\\frac{3(x-2y)-(2x+3y)(-2)}{(x-2y)^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{3x-6y+4x+6y}{(x-2y)^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{7x}{(x-2y)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing with the supplied form gives $h(x,y)=7x$. These formulas apply where $x-2y\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698825 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ for $f(x,y,z)=\\left(x + z\\right) \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + \\left(y + z\\right) \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, holding $x$ and $z$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $y$; all remaining factors stay fixed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\left(x + z\\right)\\cos y+\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\cdot 1\\\\&=\\left(x + z\\right) \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698826 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ for $f(x,y,z)=\\left(- 3 x - 3 y\\right) \\sin{\\left(z \\right)} + \\left(- 2 y - 2 z\\right) \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, holding $y$ and $z$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $x$; all remaining factors stay fixed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(z \\right)}\\cdot 1+\\left(- 2 y - 2 z\\right)\\left(-\\sin x\\right)\\\\&=\\left(2 y + 2 z\\right) \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 3 \\sin{\\left(z \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698828 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ for $f(x,y,z)=\\left(2 x + 2 z\\right) \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + \\left(3 y + 3 z\\right) \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, holding $y$ and $z$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $x$; all remaining factors stay fixed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\left(3 y + 3 z\\right)\\cos x+2 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\cdot 1\\\\&=\\left(3 y + 3 z\\right) \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698832 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ for $f(x,y,z)=\\left(- 3 x - 3 z\\right) \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + \\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right) \\cos{\\left(z \\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, holding $x$ and $z$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $y$; all remaining factors stay fixed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\left(- 3 x - 3 z\\right)\\cos y+- 2 \\cos{\\left(z \\right)}\\cdot 1\\\\&=\\left(- 3 x - 3 z\\right) \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(z \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698842 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ for $f(x,y,z)=\\left(- 2 x - 2 z\\right) \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + \\left(2 x + 2 y\\right) \\sin{\\left(z \\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, holding $x$ and $z$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $y$; all remaining factors stay fixed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=2 \\sin{\\left(z \\right)}\\cdot 1+\\left(- 2 x - 2 z\\right)\\left(-\\sin y\\right)\\\\&=\\left(2 x + 2 z\\right) \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(z \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698852 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf a=\\langle -3,-2,x\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle -3,2,0\\rangle$. Given that $|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|=\\sqrt{157}$, find all real values of $x$.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values are $x=$ {{field-1}} and $x=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, compute each signed component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\-3&-2&x\\\\-3&2&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nc_1&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2&x\\\\2&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((-2)(0)-(x)(2))=- 2x\\\\\nc_2&=-\\begin{vmatrix}-3&x\\\\-3&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=-((-3)(0)-(x)(-3))=- 3x\\\\\nc_3&=\\begin{vmatrix}-3&-2\\\\-3&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((-3)(2)-(-2)(-3))=-12\\\\\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\langle - 2x,- 3x,-12\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $N(x)$ be the sum of the squared components. Compute the magnitude, square both sides, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(x)&=(- 2x)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(- 3x)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(-12)^2\\\\\n&=13x^{2} + 144\\\\\n|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|&=\\sqrt{13x^{2} + 144}\\\\\n\\sqrt{13x^{2} + 144}&=\\sqrt{157}\\\\\n13x^{2} + 144&=157\\\\\n13x^2&=13\\\\\nx^2&=1\\\\\nx&=\\pm\\sqrt{1}\\\\\nx&=-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck each candidate in the squared magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(-1)&=157=157\\\\\nN(1)&=157=157\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $N(x)$ is the sum of the squared cross-product components. Both magnitudes are nonnegative, so these checks also verify the original magnitude equation. In ascending order, all real solutions are $x=-1$ and $x=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698857 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf a=\\langle -1,2x,-2\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 1,0,1\\rangle$. Given that $|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|=\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{3}$, find all real values of $x$.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values are $x=$ {{field-1}} and $x=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, compute each signed component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\-1&2x&-2\\\\1&0&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nc_1&=\\begin{vmatrix}2x&-2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((2x)(1)-(-2)(0))=2x\\\\\nc_2&=-\\begin{vmatrix}-1&-2\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=-((-1)(1)-(-2)(1))=-1\\\\\nc_3&=\\begin{vmatrix}-1&2x\\\\1&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((-1)(0)-(2x)(1))=- 2x\\\\\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\langle 2x,-1,- 2x\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $N(x)$ be the sum of the squared components. Compute the magnitude, square both sides, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(x)&=(2x)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(-1)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(- 2x)^2\\\\\n&=8x^{2} + 1\\\\\n|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|&=\\sqrt{8x^{2} + 1}\\\\\n\\sqrt{8x^{2} + 1}&=\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{3}\\\\\n8x^{2} + 1&=\\frac{41}{9}\\\\\n8x^2&=\\frac{32}{9}\\\\\nx^2&=\\frac{4}{9}\\\\\nx&=\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}}\\\\\nx&=- \\frac{2}{3}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck each candidate in the squared magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN\\left(- \\frac{2}{3}\\right)&=\\frac{41}{9}=\\frac{41}{9}\\\\\nN\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)&=\\frac{41}{9}=\\frac{41}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $N(x)$ is the sum of the squared cross-product components. Both magnitudes are nonnegative, so these checks also verify the original magnitude equation. In ascending order, all real solutions are $x=- \\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=\\frac{2}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698861 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf a=\\langle - 2x,2,-2\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle x,3,-1\\rangle$. Given that $|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|=4 \\sqrt{6}$, find all real values of $x$.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values are $x=$ {{field-1}} and $x=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, compute each signed component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\- 2x&2&-2\\\\x&3&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nc_1&=\\begin{vmatrix}2&-2\\\\3&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((2)(-1)-(-2)(3))=4\\\\\nc_2&=-\\begin{vmatrix}- 2x&-2\\\\x&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=-((- 2x)(-1)-(-2)(x))=- 4x\\\\\nc_3&=\\begin{vmatrix}- 2x&2\\\\x&3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((- 2x)(3)-(2)(x))=- 8x\\\\\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\langle 4,- 4x,- 8x\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $N(x)$ be the sum of the squared components. Compute the magnitude, square both sides, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(x)&=(4)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(- 4x)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(- 8x)^2\\\\\n&=80x^{2} + 16\\\\\n|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|&=\\sqrt{80x^{2} + 16}\\\\\n\\sqrt{80x^{2} + 16}&=4 \\sqrt{6}\\\\\n80x^{2} + 16&=96\\\\\n80x^2&=80\\\\\nx^2&=1\\\\\nx&=\\pm\\sqrt{1}\\\\\nx&=-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck each candidate in the squared magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(-1)&=96=96\\\\\nN(1)&=96=96\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $N(x)$ is the sum of the squared cross-product components. Both magnitudes are nonnegative, so these checks also verify the original magnitude equation. In ascending order, all real solutions are $x=-1$ and $x=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698863 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf a=\\langle -3,0,-2\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 1,x,0\\rangle$. Given that $|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|=2 \\sqrt{14}$, find all real values of $x$.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values are $x=$ {{field-1}} and $x=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, compute each signed component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\-3&0&-2\\\\1&x&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nc_1&=\\begin{vmatrix}0&-2\\\\x&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((0)(0)-(-2)(x))=2x\\\\\nc_2&=-\\begin{vmatrix}-3&-2\\\\1&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=-((-3)(0)-(-2)(1))=-2\\\\\nc_3&=\\begin{vmatrix}-3&0\\\\1&x\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((-3)(x)-(0)(1))=- 3x\\\\\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\langle 2x,-2,- 3x\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $N(x)$ be the sum of the squared components. Compute the magnitude, square both sides, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(x)&=(2x)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(-2)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(- 3x)^2\\\\\n&=13x^{2} + 4\\\\\n|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|&=\\sqrt{13x^{2} + 4}\\\\\n\\sqrt{13x^{2} + 4}&=2 \\sqrt{14}\\\\\n13x^{2} + 4&=56\\\\\n13x^2&=52\\\\\nx^2&=4\\\\\nx&=\\pm\\sqrt{4}\\\\\nx&=-2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck each candidate in the squared magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(-2)&=56=56\\\\\nN(2)&=56=56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $N(x)$ is the sum of the squared cross-product components. Both magnitudes are nonnegative, so these checks also verify the original magnitude equation. In ascending order, all real solutions are $x=-2$ and $x=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698868 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf a=\\langle -1,-3,x\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 1,2,2x\\rangle$. Given that $|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|=\\sqrt{366}$, find all real values of $x$.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values are $x=$ {{field-1}} and $x=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, compute each signed component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\-1&-3&x\\\\1&2&2x\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nc_1&=\\begin{vmatrix}-3&x\\\\2&2x\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((-3)(2x)-(x)(2))=- 8x\\\\\nc_2&=-\\begin{vmatrix}-1&x\\\\1&2x\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=-((-1)(2x)-(x)(1))=3x\\\\\nc_3&=\\begin{vmatrix}-1&-3\\\\1&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((-1)(2)-(-3)(1))=1\\\\\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\langle - 8x,3x,1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $N(x)$ be the sum of the squared components. Compute the magnitude, square both sides, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(x)&=(- 8x)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(3x)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(1)^2\\\\\n&=73x^{2} + 1\\\\\n|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|&=\\sqrt{73x^{2} + 1}\\\\\n\\sqrt{73x^{2} + 1}&=\\sqrt{366}\\\\\n73x^{2} + 1&=366\\\\\n73x^2&=365\\\\\nx^2&=5\\\\\nx&=\\pm\\sqrt{5}\\\\\nx&=- \\sqrt{5}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck each candidate in the squared magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(- \\sqrt{5})&=366=366\\\\\nN(\\sqrt{5})&=366=366\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $N(x)$ is the sum of the squared cross-product components. Both magnitudes are nonnegative, so these checks also verify the original magnitude equation. In ascending order, all real solutions are $x=- \\sqrt{5}$ and $x=\\sqrt{5}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699052 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{- 2 x^{2} - 3 y^{2} - 2}{\\sqrt{y}},\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ at the point $(-1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $x$ constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Write $N=- 2 x^{2} - 3 y^{2} - 2$ and $D=\\sqrt{y}$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{y}&=- 6 y, \\\\\nD_{y}&=\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{y}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the square root use $D_{y}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}$.  Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{N_{y}D-ND_{y}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(- 6 y\\right)D-N\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{y}}\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{2 x^{2} - 9 y^{2} + 2}{2\\left(y\\right)\\sqrt{y}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step multiplies the numerator and denominator by $2\\sqrt{y}$ and collects the numerator terms. Now substitute the point, after differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\right|_{(-1,1)}&=\\frac{\\left(-6\\right)\\left(1\\right)-\\left(-7\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(1\\right)^2} \\\\\n&=- \\frac{5}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n These expressions apply where $y>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699053 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{3 x^{2} + y^{2} - 1}{e^{x - y}},\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ at the point $(-1,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $x$ constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Write $N=3 x^{2} + y^{2} - 1$ and $D=e^{x - y}$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{y}&=2 y, \\\\\nD_{y}&=- e^{x - y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule differentiates the exponent first; its inner derivative is $-1$. Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{N_{y}D-ND_{y}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(2 y\\right)D-N\\left(- e^{x - y}\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{3 x^{2} + y^{2} + 2 y - 1}{e^{x - y}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step factors the common nonzero exponential $e^{x - y}$ from the numerator. Cancel that factor with one copy in the squared denominator. Now substitute the point, after differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\right|_{(-1,2)}&=\\frac{\\left(4\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{e^{3}}\\right)-\\left(6\\right)\\left(- \\frac{1}{e^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{e^{3}}\\right)^2} \\\\\n&=10 e^{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n These expressions apply where $e^{x - y}\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699058 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{- 2 x y - 3}{2 x - 2 y - 1},\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(0,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $y$ constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Write $N=- 2 x y - 3$ and $D=2 x - 2 y - 1$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{x}&=- 2 y, \\\\\nD_{x}&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{N_{x}D-ND_{x}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(- 2 y\\right)D-N\\left(2\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{4 y^{2} + 2 y + 6}{\\left(2 x - 2 y - 1\\right)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step distributes the subtraction and collects like terms. Now substitute the point, after differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\right|_{(0,-1)}&=\\frac{\\left(2\\right)\\left(1\\right)-\\left(-3\\right)\\left(2\\right)}{\\left(1\\right)^2} \\\\\n&=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n These expressions apply where $2 x - 2 y - 1\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699072 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{- 3 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}}{e^{- x + y}},\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(-1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $y$ constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Write $N=- 3 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}$ and $D=e^{- x + y}$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{x}&=- 6 x, \\\\\nD_{x}&=- e^{- x + y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule differentiates the exponent first; its inner derivative is $-1$. Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{N_{x}D-ND_{x}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(- 6 x\\right)D-N\\left(- e^{- x + y}\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{- 3 x^{2} - 6 x - 2 y^{2}}{e^{- x + y}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step factors the common nonzero exponential $e^{- x + y}$ from the numerator. Cancel that factor with one copy in the squared denominator. Now substitute the point, after differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\right|_{(-1,1)}&=\\frac{\\left(6\\right)\\left(e^{2}\\right)-\\left(-5\\right)\\left(- e^{2}\\right)}{\\left(e^{2}\\right)^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{e^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n These expressions apply where $e^{- x + y}\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699081 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x y - 3}{- x + 2 y},\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ at the point $(-1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $x$ constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Write $N=x y - 3$ and $D=- x + 2 y$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{y}&=x, \\\\\nD_{y}&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{N_{y}D-ND_{y}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(x\\right)D-N\\left(2\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{6 - x^{2}}{\\left(- x + 2 y\\right)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step distributes the subtraction and collects like terms. Now substitute the point, after differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\right|_{(-1,1)}&=\\frac{\\left(-1\\right)\\left(3\\right)-\\left(-4\\right)\\left(2\\right)}{\\left(3\\right)^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{9}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n These expressions apply where $- x + 2 y\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699138 :question/problem "For $f(x,y)=y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}$, evaluate $f_y(2,\\sqrt{5})$. Assume $(x,y)\\ne(0,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=1,\\quad r=0\\\\\nu&=y,\\quad v=\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_y&=1\\\\\nv_y&=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\\\\nr_y&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply both terms of the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\\\\n&=1\\left[\\left(1\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\right)+\\left(y\\right)\\left(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\right)\\right]+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{y^{2}}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}} + \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=2$ and $y=\\sqrt{5}$ and combine the exact values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(2,\\sqrt{5})&=\\left(1\\right)\\left(3\\right)+\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{14}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699144 :question/problem "For $f(x,y)=x \\tan{\\left(x y^{2} \\right)}$, evaluate $f_x(\\frac{\\pi}{16},-2)$. Assume $\\cos(x y^{2})\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=1,\\quad r=0\\\\\nu&=x,\\quad v=\\tan{\\left(x y^{2} \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_x&=1\\\\\nv_x&=y^{2} \\sec^{2}{\\left(x y^{2} \\right)}\\\\\nr_x&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply both terms of the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=C(u_xv+uv_x)+r_x\\\\\n&=1\\left[\\left(1\\right)\\left(\\tan{\\left(x y^{2} \\right)}\\right)+\\left(x\\right)\\left(y^{2} \\sec^{2}{\\left(x y^{2} \\right)}\\right)\\right]+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=x y^{2} \\sec^{2}{\\left(x y^{2} \\right)} + \\tan{\\left(x y^{2} \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi}{16}$ and $y=-2$ and combine the exact values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(\\frac{\\pi}{16},-2)&=\\left(1\\right)\\left(1\\right)+\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{16}\\right)\\left(8\\right)\\\\\n&=1 + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699145 :question/problem "For $f(x,y)=y^{2} \\tan{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}$, evaluate $f_y(-1,- \\frac{\\pi}{4})$. Assume $\\cos(x^{2} y)\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=1,\\quad r=0\\\\\nu&=y^{2},\\quad v=\\tan{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_y&=2 y\\\\\nv_y&=x^{2} \\sec^{2}{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}\\\\\nr_y&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply both terms of the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\\\\n&=1\\left[\\left(2 y\\right)\\left(\\tan{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}\\right)+\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\left(x^{2} \\sec^{2}{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}\\right)\\right]+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=x^{2} y^{2} \\sec^{2}{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)} + 2 y \\tan{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=-1$ and $y=- \\frac{\\pi}{4}$ and combine the exact values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(-1,- \\frac{\\pi}{4})&=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{16}\\right)\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{8} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699154 :question/problem "For $f(x,y)=x^{2} e^{x} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$, evaluate $f_x(2,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=\\cos{\\left(y \\right)},\\quad r=0\\\\\nu&=x^{2},\\quad v=e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_x&=2 x\\\\\nv_x&=e^{x}\\\\\nr_x&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply both terms of the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=C(u_xv+uv_x)+r_x\\\\\n&=\\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\left[\\left(2 x\\right)\\left(e^{x}\\right)+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\left(e^{x}\\right)\\right]+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(x^{2} e^{x} + 2 x e^{x}\\right) \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=2$ and $y=0$ and combine the exact values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(2,0)&=\\left(4\\right)\\left(e^{2}\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\left(e^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=8 e^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699155 :question/problem "For $f(x,y)=x y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$, evaluate $f_x(- \\pi,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=y,\\quad r=- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\\\\nu&=x,\\quad v=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_x&=1\\\\\nv_x&=- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\\\\nr_x&=- \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply both terms of the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=C(u_xv+uv_x)+r_x\\\\\n&=y\\left[\\left(1\\right)\\left(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)+\\left(x\\right)\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right]+\\left(- \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=y \\left(- x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right) - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=- \\pi$ and $y=2$ and combine the exact values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(- \\pi,2)&=2\\left[\\left(1\\right)\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(- \\pi\\right)\\left(0\\right)\\right]+\\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699474 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2 x^{2} + y^{2}}$, evaluate $f_x(1,- \\sqrt{2})$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=2 x^{2} + y^{2}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=4 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sqrt{u}\\right)&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{u}}\\right)\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{u}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(4 x\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 x}{\\sqrt{2 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=- \\sqrt{2}$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=2 \\left(1\\right)^{2} + \\left(- \\sqrt{2}\\right)^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(1,- \\sqrt{2})&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{u_0}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $(x,y)\\ne(0,0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699479 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} y^{3} + e^{- 2 x y}$, evaluate $f_x(0,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=- 2 x y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=- 2 y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(e^{u}\\right)&=\\left(e^{u}\\right)\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- 2 x^{2} y^{3}\\right)&=- 4 x y^{3}\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(e^{u}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 2 y\\right)+\\left(- 4 x y^{3}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 4 x y^{3} - 2 y e^{- 2 x y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=- 2 \\left(0\\right) \\left(1\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(0,1)&=\\left(e^{u_0}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-2\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(-2\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}]}
